Confuciusornithidae was first named by Hou ''et al.'' in 1995 to contain the type genus, ''Confuciusornis'', and assigned to the monotypic clade Confuciusornithiformes within the [[Class (biology)|class]] [[Aves]].<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Hou|first1=L|last2=Zhou|first2=Z|last3=Gu|first3=Y|last4=Zhang|first4=H|date=1995|title=Description of Confuciusornis sanctus|journal=Chinese Science Bulletin|volume=10|pages=61–63}}</ref> The group was given a [[phylogenetic]] definition by Chiappe, in 1999, who defined a node-based [[clade]] Confuciusornithidae to include only ''Changchengornis'' and ''Confuciusornis''.<ref name="Chiappe">Chiappe, Luis M. et al. (1999)</ref>

There are a number of features that define the clade. The most significant is the presence of a toothless jaw, which shows a more birdlike adaptation compared to ''Archaeopteryx''. The other defining features are as follows, according to Chiappe ''et al.'' (1999):<ref name="Chiappe" /> 
* There is a fork in the [[Rostralward|rostral]] (front) part of the [[mandibular symphysis]], the area where the mandibles fuse together.
* The presence of a distinct maxillary fenestra (breathing hole) in the [[maxilla]]'s ascending ramus.
* The [[Clavipectoral triangle|deltopectoral]] crest of the [[humerus]] is prominent, which allows for increased muscle attachment for adductor<!--shoulder, not hip--> muscles.
* The first [[Metacarpal bones|metacarpal]] is not attached by bone to the other co-ossified metacarpals.
* The claw of the second manual digit is much smaller than the other claws.
* A V-shape in the [[sternum]] in the [[Caudal (anatomical term)|caudal]] end.
* The [[Anatomical terms of location|proximal]] [[Phalanx bone|phalanx]] of the third digit is much smaller than the other phalanges.

Confuciusornithidae is the most basal group of the clade [[Pygostylia]], whose members possess a [[pygostyle]], a fused set of caudal vertebrae at the end of the tail. The pygostyle replaced the longer, unfused tail found in more primitive avialans such as ''Archaeopteryx'',<ref name="Chiappe" /> and may have served to improve flight. Pygostylia includes all modern birds, the only living members of the clade.

Additional members have been added to Confuciusornitidae since 1999. ''Jinzhouornis'' was added by Hou, Zhou, and Zhang in 2002,<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Hou|first1=L. H.|last2=Zhou|first2=Zhouhe|last3=Zhang|first3=Fucheng|last4=Gu|first4=Yucai|date=2002|title=Mesozoic birds from western Liaoning in China|journal=Liaoning Science Cretaceous of China with Information from Two New Species}}</ref> and in 2008, Zhang, Zhou and Benton assigned the newly described genus ''Eoconfuciusornis'' to the family.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Zhang|first1=FuCheng|last2=Zhou|first2=ZhongHe|last3=Benton|first3=Michael J.|date=2008-05-01|title=A primitive confuciusornithid bird from China and its implications for early avian flight|journal=Science in China Series D: Earth Sciences|language=en|volume=51|issue=5|pages=625–639|doi=10.1007/s11430-008-0050-3|bibcode=2008ScChD..51..625Z|s2cid=84157320|issn=1006-9313}}</ref>

